page":"https://registry.bazel.build/docs/rules_nodejs","size":39722,"stargazers_count":753,"watchers_count":753,"language":"Starlark","has_issues":true,"has_projects":false,"has_downloads":false,"has_wiki":true,"has_pages":true,"has_discussions":true,"forks_count":524,"mirror_url":null,"archived":false,"disabled":false,"open_issues_count":14,"license":{"key":"apache-2.0","name":"Apache License 2.0","spdx_id":"Apache-2.0","url":"https://api.github.com/licenses/apache-2.0","node_id":"MDc6TGljZW5zZTI="},"allow_forking":true,"is_template":false,"web_commit_signoff_required":false,"topics":["aspect-build","bazel","bazel-rules","nodejs"],"visibility":"public","forks":524,"open_issues":14,"watchers":753,"default_branch":"main","permissions":{"admin":false,"maintain":false,"push":false,"triage":false,"pull":true}},"body":"# 🐞 bug report\r\n\r\n### Affected Rule\r\n\r\nAny rule using the linker -- I linked a repo below that has a small webpack wrapper using the `run_node` helper.\r\n\r\n### Is this a regression?\r\n\r\nNo\r\n\r\n### Description\r\n\r\nWhen a rule using the linker is not sandboxed, it will create a `node_modules` symlink in `execroot/$wksp`. When not sandboxed, this may be itself be a symlink to the workspace's `node_modules`, or a symlink to `external/npm/node_modules` (which is then a symlink to the one in the workspace).\r\n\r\nThis can unfortunately be a huge problem if recursive globs are used in the package containing the `node_modules` directory, because of https://github.com/bazelbuild/bazel/issues/7433.\r\nThe fix for this hasn't made it to any releases yet, so _even though_ `node_modules` is in `.bazelignore`, this symlink can cause Bazel to exhaustively recurse through the entire `bazel-bin` directory.\r\n\r\nI've seen this fail in a few ways: an output with runfiles that contain a symlink to the workspace (causing an \"infinite symlink expansion\" error), or it recurses so much that it exhausts the Bazel server's heap space.\r\n\r\n## 🔬 Minimal Reproduction\r\n\r\nhttps://github.com/clintharrison/nodejs_linker_sandboxing has a small rule that wraps webpack to use the linker.\r\n\r\n`bazel build --spawn_strategy=standalone //...` creates some symlinks in the root `node_modules`, but will succeed the first time.\r\n\r\nRun it again, and it fails.\r\n\r\nThis is a bit contrived, and it's tempting to say \"don't use `--spawn_strategy=standalone`\", but the default for persistent workers is to run without sandboxing, and it's seemingly unlikely for that default to change any time soon, per https://github.com/bazelbuild/bazel/issues/6111.\r\n\r\n## 🔥 Exception or Error\r\n\r\n<pre><code>\r\n❯ bazel build --spawn_strategy=standalone //...\r\nINFO: Writing tracer profile to '/private/var/tmp/_bazel_charrison/59e32b2662cefe402a6eec94fa20ce25/command.profile.gz'\r\nERROR: /Users/charrison/code/tmp/nodejs_linker_sandboxing/app/BUILD.bazel:3:1: in srcs attribute of py_binary rule //app:app: file name 'src/app.py' specified by 'main' attribute matches multiple files: e.g., 'app/node_modules/nodejs_linker_sandboxing/app/app.runfiles/nodejs_linker_sandboxing/app/src/app.py' and 'app/src/app.py'\r\nERROR: Analysis of target '//app:app' failed; build aborted: Analysis of target '//app:app' failed; build aborted\r\nINFO: Elapsed time: 0.111s\r\nINFO: 0 processes.\r\nFAILED: Build did NOT complete successfully (1 packages loaded, 6 targets configured)\r\n</code></pre>\r\n\r\n\r\n## 🌍  Your Environment\r\n\r\n**Operating System:**\r\n\r\n<pre><code>\r\nProductName:\tMac OS X\r\nProductVersion:\t10.14.4\r\nBuildVersion:\t18E226\r\n</code></pre>\r\n\r\n**Output of `bazel version`:**\r\n\r\n<pre><code>\r\nBuild label: 1.1.0\r\nBuild target: bazel-out/darwin-opt/bin/src/main/java/com/google/devtools/build/lib/bazel/BazelServer_deploy.jar\r\nBuild time: Mon Oct 21 08:47:13 2019 (1571647633)\r\nBuild timestamp: 1571647633\r\nBuild timestamp as int: 1571647633\r\n</code></pre>\r\n\r\n**Rules version (SHA):**\r\n\r\nRelease tar, 0.39.1\r\n\r\n**Anything else relevant?**\r\n\r\nI mentioned this in Slack; primarily creating this to track the issue.","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/bazel-contrib/rules_nodejs/issues/1340/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/bazel-contrib/rules_nodejs/issues/1340/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":"completed"}},"event":"cross-referenced"},{"id":3444114633,"node_id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDM0NDQxMTQ2MzM=","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/bazelbuild/bazel/issues/events/3444114633","actor":{"login":"philwo","id":504652,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUwNDY1Mg==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/504652?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/philwo","html_url":"https://github.com/philwo","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/philwo/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/philwo/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/philwo/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/philwo/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/philwo/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/philwo/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/philwo/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/philwo/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/philwo/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"event":"labeled","commit_id":null,"commit_url":null,"created_at":"2020-06-15T12:17:33Z","label":{"name":"team-XProduct","color":"f1f442"},"performed_via_github_app":null},{"id":5684870591,"node_id":"UNLE_lADOATz7jc4YekSBzwAAAAFS2D2_","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/bazelbuild/bazel/issues/events/5684870591","actor":{"login":"philwo","id":504652,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUwNDY1Mg==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/504652?v=4